\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Whitney forms on a smooth triangulation}
\date{2026-11-15}

\p{Over #{\mathbb R}, let the manifold be compact, oriented and
Riemannian, with a smooth triangulation and the relative
boundary conditions of the paper. Write #{\eta_m} for the mesh
of the standard subdivision.}

\p{The Whitney map #{\Wh} sends a real cochain to an
#{\mathrm L^2} form, and the de Rham map #{\Rh} integrates a
form over a chain. The Whitney map intertwines the simplicial
coboundary with the exterior derivative,
##{\Wh\,\extd\,c=\extd\,\Wh\,c,}
by \citet{(1.4), p. 82}{dodziuk1976hodge}.
Integrating that form over a simplicial chain recovers the
pairing of the cochain with the chain, by
\citet{(1.7), p. 84}{dodziuk1976hodge}.}

\p{\citet{Theorem 3.7, p. 89}{dodziuk1976hodge}
states a pointwise bound. For a smooth #{p}-form #{f} there is a
constant #{C_f}, independent of the subdivision, such that
##{\|f(x)-{\Wh}_{m}{\Rh}_{m} f(x)\|_x\le C_f\eta_m}
almost everywhere. The subscript is the point.}

\p{\citet{Definition 4.1, p. 93}{dodziuk1976hodge}
sets the inner product of two cochains equal to the #{\mathrm L^2}
inner product of their Whitney forms. For a smooth form whose
de Rham image lies in the relative cochains,
\citet{Theorem 4.9, p. 95}{dodziuk1976hodge}
sends the three summands of the combinatorial Hodge
decomposition to the three summands of the smooth Hodge
decomposition, with convergence in #{\mathrm L^2}.}

\p{\citet{Theorem 5.7, p. 99}{dodziuk1976hodge}
compares eigenvalues of #{- \covsq} on functions, the positive
Laplacian. For each positive integer #{i} at most the dimension
of the finite function space,
##{\lambda_i^{(m)}-C_i\eta_m\le\lambda_i\le\lambda_i^{(m)}.}
The constant #{C_i} depends on the index and not on the
subdivision.}

\p{Whitney constructs the map from flat cochains to forms on
a simplicial complex over #{\mathbb R}, in Chapter VII,
section 11, pp. 226–229
\citet{(11.1), p. 226}{whitney1957geometric}.
The map commutes with the coboundary,
##{\Wh\,\extd=\extd\,\Wh,}
\citet{(11.4), p. 227}{whitney1957geometric},
and integrating the form recovers the cochain,
#{\Rh\Wh=\bbI}
\citet{(11.5), p. 227}{whitney1957geometric}.
\citet{(11.2), p. 227}{whitney1957geometric}
records that the integration commutes with the differential.
The construction is simplicial. It supplies no cubical
complex and no counting product.}
